let's say starting at 0.

494
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:35,420
Then you're looking
at the first element.

495
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:37,500
If you're summing here--

496
00:40:37,500 --> 00:40:41,300
let's say you're summing
from 1 to infinity.

497
00:40:41,300 --> 00:40:44,610
If you're summing from 1--
then the first element here--

498
00:40:44,610 --> 00:40:46,110
this is minus 1.

499
00:40:46,110 --> 00:40:48,630
This is non-negative, so
this is a negative number.

500
00:40:48,630 --> 00:40:50,030
So you're going down.

501
00:40:50,030 --> 00:40:51,260
You're starting at 0.

502
00:40:51,260 --> 00:40:53,720
You're going down

503
00:40:53,720 --> 00:40:59,440
The next element-- that will be
positive because it's 2 here.

504
00:40:59,440 --> 00:41:02,920
So it's positive--
it would go up.

505
00:41:02,920 --> 00:41:06,420
But it's monotone, so
you don't go as much

506
00:41:06,420 --> 00:41:08,220
up as you're going down.

507
00:41:08,220 --> 00:41:10,900
So you're first-- first
you're going down,

508
00:41:10,900 --> 00:41:12,070
then you're going up.

509
00:41:12,070 --> 00:41:15,240
But you're going less up,e
then you're going less down,

510
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:17,880
then you're going
less up, et cetera.

511
00:41:17,880 --> 00:41:20,585
And that actually is
forcing it to converge.

512
00:41:23,454 --> 00:41:26,205
And so now why is this related
to your question about,

513
00:41:26,205 --> 00:41:28,030
well, how do you
reconcile these?

514
00:41:28,030 --> 00:41:30,990
So you see, if you're looking
at something like this

515
00:41:30,990 --> 00:41:34,910
and you now took all of the--

516
00:41:34,910 --> 00:41:40,890
if you first took many, many of
the positive elements and then

517
00:41:40,890 --> 00:41:43,920
one or two of the
negative and then many

518
00:41:43,920 --> 00:41:48,630
of the positive elements again,
then actually, you could--

519
00:41:48,630 --> 00:41:50,970
for this series,
if you rearrange

520
00:41:50,970 --> 00:41:52,710
the order of the
things you're summing,

521
00:41:52,710 --> 00:41:56,390
and then you could
either get it to diverge,

522
00:41:56,390 --> 00:41:59,260
or you could get it to converge
to pretty much anything you

523
00:41:59,260 --> 00:41:59,760
want.

524
00:42:02,590 --> 00:42:05,980
So this hopefully
explains why when

525
00:42:05,980 --> 00:42:11,500
the series is not non-negative
numbers, why the ordering really

526
00:42:11,500 --> 00:42:12,140
matters.

527
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:16,780
And again, this is very
closely related to this

528
00:42:16,780 --> 00:42:18,350
with the product of things.

529
00:42:18,350 --> 00:42:19,880
Why it's related?

530
00:42:19,880 --> 00:42:25,090
Because we're getting
this remainder term.

531
00:42:25,090 --> 00:42:28,570
We've got something that
was like the remainder,

532
00:42:28,570 --> 00:42:30,910
and it's the remainder
that you want

533
00:42:30,910 --> 00:42:34,330
to be small in order to say
that things are converging

534
00:42:34,330 --> 00:42:35,540
to the same thing.

535
00:42:35,540 --> 00:42:41,110
And you wouldn't have that
necessarily in general

536
00:42:41,110 --> 00:42:45,010
unless you're assuming that
the terms were non-negative.

537
00:42:45,010 --> 00:42:49,600
So let's come back to-- that was
just a little bit of [? side, ?]

538
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:53,300
but I hope it makes
things a bit more clear.

539
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:55,920
Let's come back to
this theorem here.

540
00:42:55,920 --> 00:42:57,750
How do we prove this?

541
00:42:57,750 --> 00:43:00,260
So we're going to prove
it from the theorem

542
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:04,520
that we did with that if you--

543
00:43:04,520 --> 00:43:09,860
so we're going to use this
theorem we just proved,

544
00:43:09,860 --> 00:43:11,070
which is this theorem here.

545
00:43:11,070 --> 00:43:12,695
So we're going to
use this theorem here

546
00:43:12,695 --> 00:43:16,730
to prove that theorem up there.

547
00:43:16,730 --> 00:43:21,333
So now it's just a matter of
identifying the right series.

548
00:43:26,540 --> 00:43:35,750
So this is what
we want to prove,

549
00:43:35,750 --> 00:43:39,510
this is what we want to
show, and this one here--

550
00:43:39,510 --> 00:43:47,210
this is the infinite sum i
equal to 0 to infinity x plus y

551
00:43:47,210 --> 00:43:48,740
to the power n--

552
00:43:48,740 --> 00:43:53,110
sorry, over-- i--

553
00:43:57,280 --> 00:44:03,790
let's call it the sum
from n, like that.

554
00:44:03,790 --> 00:44:06,090
That's what that series is.

555
00:44:08,710 --> 00:44:11,140
And this one here--

556
00:44:11,140 --> 00:44:17,230
that's the sum x n, n
factorial, and this one here

557
00:44:17,230 --> 00:44:25,450
is sum y n, n factorial.

558
00:44:25,450 --> 00:44:33,860
Now, so if I want cn--

559
00:44:33,860 --> 00:44:39,640
so the idea is that if
I'm writing cn as x plus y

560
00:44:39,640 --> 00:44:43,150
to the power n
over n factorial--

561
00:44:43,150 --> 00:44:46,390
suppose I write cn
like this, and I

562
00:44:46,390 --> 00:44:53,150
write a n x n over
n factorial, and I

563
00:44:53,150 --> 00:44:59,600
write bn y n over n factorial.

564
00:44:59,600 --> 00:45:03,390
Suppose I define them like this.

565
00:45:03,390 --> 00:45:06,180
I already know that
this here is convergent

566
00:45:06,180 --> 00:45:08,310
and that here is convergent.

567
00:45:08,310 --> 00:45:09,800
By the way, of
course, I also know

568
00:45:09,800 --> 00:45:12,780
that this here is convergent.

569
00:45:12,780 --> 00:45:16,970
But I now have that--

570
00:45:16,970 --> 00:45:19,250
but I don't have--

571
00:45:19,250 --> 00:45:23,660
I haven't proved--
but I claim now

572
00:45:23,660 --> 00:45:29,470
that this cn is really
formed in this way.

573
00:45:29,470 --> 00:45:30,420
So let's see.

574
00:45:30,420 --> 00:45:38,240
So cn here-- if I define
cn to be this thing here,

575
00:45:38,240 --> 00:45:40,590
by the binomial formula--

576
00:45:40,590 --> 00:45:43,220
so x plus y--

577
00:45:43,220 --> 00:45:46,130
if you take this
here to the power n,

578
00:45:46,130 --> 00:45:55,980
this thing here is the sum
here of x i times y n minus i.

579
00:45:55,980 --> 00:45:58,830
And then the coefficient is--

580
00:45:58,830 --> 00:46:07,770
sorry-- x i times y to the n
minus y, i equal to 0 through n.

581
00:46:07,770 --> 00:46:11,770
And the coefficient
is n choose i.

582
00:46:11,770 --> 00:46:13,280
That's the binomial formula.

583
00:46:29,916 --> 00:46:35,670
Now, what is n choose i?

584
00:46:35,670 --> 00:46:40,740
Well, n choose i--

585
00:46:40,740 --> 00:46:53,550
this is just n times n minus
1 down to n minus i plus 1.

586
00:46:53,550 --> 00:46:58,790
This gives you n elements, and
then divided by i factorial.

587
00:47:02,900 --> 00:47:07,230
But you can write this
thing here as n factorial.

588
00:47:07,230 --> 00:47:10,770
If you do that, then
you included too many,

589
00:47:10,770 --> 00:47:17,100
so you have to divide
by n minus i factorial.

590
00:47:17,100 --> 00:47:20,930
And then, of course, you
still have the i factorial

591
00:47:20,930 --> 00:47:25,790
in the denominator, so
you can write it like that

592
00:47:25,790 --> 00:47:29,450
So this means that the cn here--

593
00:47:29,450 --> 00:47:36,830
our cn is-- so we defined cn
like this-- x to the power

594
00:47:36,830 --> 00:47:41,261
plus y to the power
n over n factorial.

595
00:47:45,180 --> 00:47:48,750
And if we use the
binomial formula,

596
00:47:48,750 --> 00:47:50,680
then it was equal to that.

597
00:47:50,680 --> 00:47:57,480
So this here was equal to 1 over
n factorial sum i equal to 0

598
00:47:57,480 --> 00:48:07,770
to n x i y n minus i, and
then n choose i-- like that.

599
00:48:07,770 --> 00:48:13,800
Now I write in this
little equation here.

600
00:48:13,800 --> 00:48:19,800
And so I get that 1 over
n factorial summing from i

601
00:48:19,800 --> 00:48:28,140
equal to 0 through n, and
then n factorial, n minus i

602
00:48:28,140 --> 00:48:35,040
factorial times i
factorial, x to the i times

603
00:48:35,040 --> 00:48:37,920
y to the n minus i.

604
00:48:37,920 --> 00:48:42,220
And now you see that
the n factorials here

605
00:48:42,220 --> 00:48:44,410
cancel with the n factorials.

606
00:48:44,410 --> 00:48:50,770
So you're left with i
equal to 0 through n.

607
00:48:50,770 --> 00:48:52,970
And now I can group it.

608
00:48:52,970 --> 00:48:54,080
I can group it.

609
00:48:54,080 --> 00:49:04,270
I can write it as x i divided
by i factorial times y

610
00:49:04,270 --> 00:49:09,290
over n minus i factorial.

611
00:49:09,290 --> 00:49:14,530
And this is to the power
n minus i, like that.

612
00:49:14,530 --> 00:49:23,130
But now you see that this here
is exactly when we defined

613
00:49:23,130 --> 00:49:25,970
a n like this and bn like this.

614
00:49:25,970 --> 00:49:31,030
This here is exactly the
sum from i equal to 0

615
00:49:31,030 --> 00:49:36,600
through n of a i b n minus i.

616
00:49:39,270 --> 00:49:45,870
And so you see that what we
have is that this thing here--

617
00:49:45,870 --> 00:49:49,560
so E of x plus y--

618
00:49:49,560 --> 00:49:52,740
with our definition
of the c's, this

619
00:49:52,740 --> 00:49:56,345
was just defined to be
equal to this limit.

620
00:49:58,890 --> 00:50:04,620
But the cn's is also given
by the product of these two

621
00:50:04,620 --> 00:50:06,180
other series.

622
00:50:06,180 --> 00:50:12,030
But this means that it must
be the product of the two,

623
00:50:12,030 --> 00:50:14,670
like that.

624
00:50:14,670 --> 00:50:21,970
It's the product of the two
sums, infinite sums, like that.

625
00:50:21,970 --> 00:50:27,520
But this thing here is,
of course, just E x,

626
00:50:27,520 --> 00:50:31,270
and this is just E y.

627
00:50:31,270 --> 00:50:32,370
So this proved it.

628
00:50:37,041 --> 00:50:41,500
And again, this here was just
assuming that the x and y

629
00:50:41,500 --> 00:50:42,770
are non-negative.

630
00:50:42,770 --> 00:50:45,070
But given what I
said-- also, this

631
00:50:45,070 --> 00:50:48,040
corollary-- it's not
very hard to prove

632
00:50:48,040 --> 00:50:49,520
the general case from that.

633
00:50:52,490 --> 00:50:56,530
So now-- but I won't do that.

634
00:50:56,530 --> 00:51:05,180
So let's just talk a little bit
more about continuous functions.

635
00:51:05,180 --> 00:51:08,210
So let me one more time
just remind you about it,

636
00:51:08,210 --> 00:51:18,105
and then let me give you a
wild example of a function.

637
00:51:22,225 --> 00:51:27,100
So first let me remind
you with continuous.

638
00:51:27,100 --> 00:51:30,250
So continuous [INAUDIBLE] is a
really important concept, also,

639
00:51:30,250 --> 00:51:32,320
in this class.

640
00:51:32,320 --> 00:51:37,160
So I have a function, and it
doesn't need to be defined.

641
00:51:37,160 --> 00:51:43,370
It's defined, say A on
some subset of R into R.

642
00:51:43,370 --> 00:51:45,810
And then we say that--

643
00:51:45,810 --> 00:51:50,490
and then we have some
point x0 in the domain.

644
00:51:50,490 --> 00:52:12,783
And we say that f here
is continuous at x0

645
00:52:12,783 --> 00:52:13,700
if the following hold.

646
00:52:13,700 --> 00:52:17,210
That's for all epsilon.

647
00:52:17,210 --> 00:52:27,020
For all epsilon greater than
0, there exists delta greater

648
00:52:27,020 --> 00:52:38,320
than 0 such that if x is in
the domain and x minus x0

649
00:52:38,320 --> 00:52:47,140
is less than delta,
then the images

650
00:52:47,140 --> 00:52:50,730
deviate at most by epsilon.

651
00:52:50,730 --> 00:52:54,500
So this is, again, what
it says to be continuous.

652
00:52:54,500 --> 00:52:57,370
And here, unlike
the previous couple

653
00:52:57,370 --> 00:53:01,250
of times when I defined it,
here it's really defined.

654
00:53:01,250 --> 00:53:05,150
You see it makes total
sense on any subset of R.

655
00:53:05,150 --> 00:53:09,250
It doesn't need to
be an interval, even.

656
00:53:09,250 --> 00:53:11,150
So it doesn't need
to be all of R,

657
00:53:11,150 --> 00:53:12,720
but it does need
to be an interval.

658
00:53:17,830 --> 00:53:23,720
Now, so this is what it means
for a function to be continuous.

659
00:53:23,720 --> 00:53:27,045
Let me give you an example of a
sort of somewhat wild function.

660
00:53:30,550 --> 00:53:35,730
And so continuous is
a very nice property.

661
00:53:35,730 --> 00:53:40,340
It's continuous if it's
continuous at all points.

662
00:53:40,340 --> 00:53:44,690
Usually, continuous is
a nice property to have.

663
00:53:44,690 --> 00:53:48,090
Often, it's the bare minimum.

664
00:53:48,090 --> 00:53:49,850
It's often not enough.

665
00:53:49,850 --> 00:53:52,940
You want something
more than continuous.

666
00:53:52,940 --> 00:54:01,850
But of course, other times,
you may have much less.

667
00:54:01,850 --> 00:54:03,093
So here's an example.

668
00:54:07,970 --> 00:54:10,620
So this is an example.

669
00:54:10,620 --> 00:54:14,600
So it's a function
defined on all of R--

670
00:54:14,600 --> 00:54:27,590
example of a function
defined on all of R

671
00:54:27,590 --> 00:54:32,236
that is nowhere continuous.

672
00:54:42,540 --> 00:54:46,140
By "nowhere continuous," I mean
that there's not a single point

673
00:54:46,140 --> 00:54:49,375
where it's continuous.

674
00:54:49,375 --> 00:54:52,920
And so this is the
function f of x.

675
00:54:52,920 --> 00:54:55,680
And the function is--

676
00:54:55,680 --> 00:55:05,880
if x here is a rational
point, then f of x is 0.

677
00:55:05,880 --> 00:55:15,420
And if x here is an irrational
point, then [? I'm ?] setting it

678
00:55:15,420 --> 00:55:16,730
equal to 1.

679
00:55:19,410 --> 00:55:21,420
So it's a pretty
simple function.

680
00:55:21,420 --> 00:55:26,820
And so I claim that this is
not continuous at any point.

681
00:55:26,820 --> 00:55:28,865
And so the thing is that--

682
00:55:35,694 --> 00:55:38,290
you see, if you take
any points-- let's just

683
00:55:38,290 --> 00:55:40,230
take a rational point first.

684
00:55:44,530 --> 00:55:49,690
So suppose you take a
rational point here.

685
00:55:49,690 --> 00:55:52,515
This here is-- x0 is rational.

686
00:56:00,280 --> 00:56:03,080
Then you have that--

687
00:56:03,080 --> 00:56:07,430
if you are looking
at square root of 2,

688
00:56:07,430 --> 00:56:11,240
we know that this
here is not rational.

689
00:56:11,240 --> 00:56:12,105
We proved that.

690
00:56:17,920 --> 00:56:20,690
We showed that this
is not rational.

691
00:56:20,690 --> 00:56:27,070
And so in fact, if
you take 1 over--

692
00:56:27,070 --> 00:56:30,340
sorry, square root
of 2 divided by n,

693
00:56:30,340 --> 00:56:33,850
where n here is
sufficiently large,

694
00:56:33,850 --> 00:56:37,190
then you can make this
thing here very close to 0.

695
00:56:39,820 --> 00:56:42,220
And then you could
add this thing here.

696
00:56:42,220 --> 00:56:49,530
So you could look at x0, and you
could look at square root of 2

697
00:56:49,530 --> 00:56:53,880
over n for n really large.

698
00:56:53,880 --> 00:56:56,980
If n is really large, you
can make this thing here,

699
00:56:56,980 --> 00:57:01,810
so this is square
root of 2 plus x0.

700
00:57:01,810 --> 00:57:05,880
So you could make this
really, really close to x0.

701
00:57:05,880 --> 00:57:09,210
This thing here
would be irrational,

702
00:57:09,210 --> 00:57:13,350
and so the value of the
function here would be 1.

703
00:57:13,350 --> 00:57:15,533
Let's make this a
little bit more precise.

704
00:57:19,980 --> 00:57:23,430
And so-- let me
maybe say it just

705
00:57:23,430 --> 00:57:25,030
before I make it more precise.

706
00:57:25,030 --> 00:57:28,060
So let's just draw
the graph here.

707
00:57:28,060 --> 00:57:31,960
So we have here x0.

708
00:57:31,960 --> 00:57:33,800
We have here this--

709
00:57:33,800 --> 00:57:36,610
this here is this
irrational number,

710
00:57:36,610 --> 00:57:38,530
which you could
think about, but it

711
00:57:38,530 --> 00:57:39,920
doesn't need to be this number.

712
00:57:39,920 --> 00:57:42,470
It could be anything
that is irrational.

713
00:57:42,470 --> 00:57:44,510
This one here is irrational.

714
00:57:44,510 --> 00:57:48,940
And by making n super
large, this here super close

715
00:57:48,940 --> 00:57:54,010
to x0, the value of
the function here is 0.

716
00:57:54,010 --> 00:57:58,170
The value of the
function here is 1.

717
00:58:01,480 --> 00:58:05,050
And so you see that you
have these points as

718
00:58:05,050 --> 00:58:08,210
close to this point
here as you want,

719
00:58:08,210 --> 00:58:11,580
but the value of the
function is very different

720
00:58:11,580 --> 00:58:22,210
So again, just to spell
it out, given an epsilon--

721
00:58:27,940 --> 00:58:37,730
so you see that if epsilon
here was smaller than 1,

722
00:58:37,730 --> 00:58:45,180
then you would have that
f of x minus f of x0--

723
00:58:45,180 --> 00:58:48,780
this here is a rational point.

724
00:58:48,780 --> 00:58:51,960
So this here is--

725
00:58:51,960 --> 00:58:57,140
this is 0, so this
is just f of x.

726
00:58:57,140 --> 00:59:04,670
And if this here is irrational--
so if x here is irrational,

727
00:59:04,670 --> 00:59:07,580
then this here is 1.

728
00:59:07,580 --> 00:59:14,720
So you would have that you can
choose this irrational point.

729
00:59:14,720 --> 00:59:35,500
So for any delta, we can
choose x, which is irrational,

730
00:59:35,500 --> 00:59:42,040
and so that x minus x0 is
smaller than delta, but we have

731
00:59:42,040 --> 00:59:49,100
that f of x here is
actually equal to 1.

732
00:59:49,100 --> 00:59:50,980
But this difference
here was supposed

733
00:59:50,980 --> 00:59:53,755
to be smaller than delta.

734
00:59:53,755 --> 00:59:55,360
That is not the case.

735
00:59:58,100 --> 01:00:02,030
If epsilon is smaller than
1, it's definitely not.

736
01:00:02,030 --> 01:00:02,980
It's 1.

737
01:00:08,590 --> 01:00:13,460
And so similarly, if you
take-- so this was proving--

738
01:00:13,460 --> 01:00:37,340
so this showed-- so this proof
proved that f is discontinuous

739
01:00:37,340 --> 01:00:40,380
at all rational points.

